**c)** Modular programming splits a program into independent, reusable functions with clear interfaces. Functions reduce duplication, isolate changes, make testing easier, and keep `main()` readable.

**a)**
```c
int gcd(int a, int b) {
    while (b != 0) { int t = a % b; a = b; b = t; }
    return a < 0 ? -a : a;
}
```

**b)** `auto`: default block-local automatic storage. `register`: hint for fast local storage. `static`: local variables persist between calls; file-scope names have internal linkage. `extern`: declares a name defined elsewhere. Example: `static int count;` inside a function retains its value across calls.

**a)** A C string is a `char` array terminated by the null character `'\0'`.

**b)** `<string.h>` examples: `strlen(s)` returns length; `strcpy(dst, src)` copies; `strcat(dst, src)` appends; `strcmp(a, b)` compares lexicographically.

```c
char d[40] = "Hello";
printf("%zu\n", strlen(d));
strcat(d, " World");
printf("%d\n", strcmp(d, "Hello World"));
```

**a)**
```c
void reverse(char s[]) {
    int i = 0, j = 0;
    while (s[j]) j++;
    for (j--; i < j; i++, j--) {
        char t = s[i]; s[i] = s[j]; s[j] = t;
    }
}
```

**b)** `auto`, `register`, `static`, and `extern` control lifetime, visibility, and storage. Example: `void f(void) { static int hits = 0; hits++; }` preserves `hits` between calls.

